//
// mesh3d — 3D geometry primitives for loft.
//
// Aggregates three sub-modules that previously lived inside the
// `graphics` package as internal submodules:
//
//   - math  — Vec2 / Vec3 / Vec4 + Mat4 + vector/matrix ops
//   - mesh  — Vertex / Triangle / Mesh + sphere generation +
//             flatten-to-floats helpers for GL upload
//   - scene — Scene / Node / Material / Camera / Light + scene
//             graph construction
//
// Promoting these into a standalone package lets non-rendering
// consumers (asset processors, glb readers, headless physics)
// use the types without pulling in `graphics`'s OpenGL stack.
//
// Public re-exports below — consumers do `use mesh3d;` and access
// `Vec3`, `Mesh`, `Scene`, etc. at the top level.  Internally each
// submodule is still importable individually
// (`use math;` / `use mesh;` / `use scene;`) for cases where only
// one sub-surface is needed.
//
// --- Using it ---
//
// A scene is three index spaces — meshes, materials, nodes — and a node is a
// pairing of one of each with a transform:
//
//     sc = mesh3d::scene("hall");
//     mi = sc.add_mesh(mesh3d::cube());
//     ma = sc.add_material(mesh3d::material_color("brass", 0.8, 0.6, 0.2));
//
//     xf = mesh3d::mat4_mul(mesh3d::mat4_translate(0.0, 1.0, 0.0),
//                           mesh3d::mat4_scale(2.0, 2.0, 2.0));
//     sc.add_node(mesh3d::node_at("crate", mi, ma, xf));
//     sc.add_light(mesh3d::directional_light("sun", 1.0, 1.0, 0.9, 3.0,
//                                            mesh3d::vec3(0.0, -1.0, 0.0)));
//
//     mesh3d::mat4_transform(xf, mesh3d::vec3(1.0, 0.0, 0.0));  // -> (2, 1, 0)
//     mesh3d::mesh_to_floats(mesh3d::cube());   // -> 216 floats, ready for GL
//
// Every `add_*` answers the INDEX the scene refers to that thing by, and those
// indices are what a node carries — so a mesh or a material is shared by naming
// it twice, never by copying it.
//
// Matrices are COLUMN-MAJOR, matching OpenGL and glTF, and `mat4_mul(a, b)`
// applies b first: the composition above scales, then translates.  Nothing here
// touches a GPU — `glb` writes a scene out, `graphics` draws one.

use math;
use mesh;
use scene;
